title Two records out of the dead-letter box, two laps deep, and a shelf that misfiled its own kin
prompt The baton, unattended lap 4095 on grain-grass. Rota row 0 by count -- Aether, the row that hears.
rota lap N 4095, N mod 5 = 0, row 0 -- Aether - Jupiter, why the work exists; threshold aether-the-row-that-hears, then follow-our-compass, the-lindy-effect-and-the-long-return, the-grain-and-the-crossing
think Aether's threshold ends on a strand seated at the fusion: a guard that cannot red guards nothing -- a witness must be proven able to make a sound before its silence means anything. The round open's first line named exactly that shape in my own tree, so the row and the work agreed before I had chosen either.
think The round open said two session logs stood in the dead-letter box and nowhere else. The cold pass then answered with two reds, stash_record and standing_equipment, and standing_equipment's evidence file named stash_record as the red it had read. Two reds, one root, and the root was a stash.
think stash@{0} was my own seat's work, 22 files and 919 insertions, made at the round open twenty minutes before I read it. Reading its two records showed a chain rather than a single miss: lap 4085 died before its commit, lap 4091 read that record back out and prepared the landing, and then lap 4091 died before ITS commit too. One stash held both packages.
think I took the content rather than the diff, which is the move lap 4091's own log recommends. Sixteen of the twenty-two files were untouched between the stash's base and HEAD, so git checkout from the stash lands them byte for byte with their modes intact -- the tree object carries the mode where the blob does not. The six that overlapped are all living pins, and I rewrote those edits by hand on today's bytes rather than resolving a conflict in the three files where a wrong resolution is least visible.
think Both numbers had moved again. The parked rows were booked at %506, renumbered to %508 by the lap that read them back out, and %508 and %509 both stand published upstream now for peers' defects. reds_spine_derive_scan --next answered 510, then 512 after two more peers published mid-send, so the pair lands at %511 and %512 -- an unshared row renumbering for the THIRD time in one round, which is the derived spine working rather than failing. Three re-integrations, which is the rota's own limit reached exactly.
obs The shelf written to record two guards that could not see their subjects carried the defect of a third row. Its Kin line read an anchor of %505 over a link to REDS-the-verdict-travelled-and-the-reason-did-not-rows-494.md, and that shelf holds zero occurrences of %505 -- the row is still living on the pin. That is exactly %502's class, a number and a path naming two rows, inside the document folding two narrowed-reach guards. Repointed to ../REDS.md before it shipped.
obs I copied a number that was never mine. Writing the live front I put 1,107 insertions already GREEN into my own account; that figure belongs to %507's stash, one block up the same card, and mine is 919. Caught by measuring rather than by reading, which is the only way this class is ever caught -- %341's borrowed number, and the card is where borrowed numbers go to look native.
obs The reach guard refused me for a promise I had not yet kept. reds_fold runs readme_reach_scan.sh, which read broken_in_living=1 and named the one link: the Prior-laps row I had just written, pointing at this log before this log existed. That is the correct refusal and the cheapest possible one -- a card citing a file is a promise, and the guard reads the promise the moment it is made rather than after the send.
obs The operator card fought its bound the whole way. My first draft of the block put the card at 41,393 against 40,960, and four condensing passes brought it to 40,948 -- twelve bytes of headroom for eight ships. The elder GRASS block it replaced was already folded word for word to its own shelf, so nothing was lost; what the bound actually taxes is the writing, and the next lap on this card starts a judgment call ahead.
obs Both instruments read GREEN on today's tree rather than on the tree they were written against. tame_style_long_fn holds its twenty planted legs and reads 321 functions over 70 lines across 611 files, longest 809; two_rooms_doorway holds its fourteen and reads 973 pages, 640 of them on folded shelves, at exactly its published ceiling of 46. A witness proven on a tree two hours stale is a claim, so both were rerun before the pins moved.
obs The blind ledger was being published on the front door, and I found it by refusing to ship a number I did not understand. Regenerating README.md's metrics block dropped fascia from 68 to 58, which is a large move for one commit, so I read the metric rather than committing it. tools/fixtures/f/fascia_metric_v0.sh calls tame_style_long_fn_one.sh per roster file and spends the count straight into clutter -- so the root README, the most Lindy-exposed page this project owns, carried a connective-tissue grade ten points above the tree it described, for as long as the ledger has been blind. Nothing about the tree changed today; only what the instrument could count.
obs And that signal saturates, which is the same class one level up. The over-70 penalty is capped at 25, so the published grade cannot tell 26 long functions from 321, and every step of the migrate-on-touch ratchet is invisible to the front door until the count falls under 25. A capped signal reports health it has not measured -- the pair's own lesson arriving a third time, inside the metric that reads them.
obs What the box costs is now measurable rather than arguable. Two laps of finished, green work -- two instruments, two controls, two scans, two records -- sat unlanded for two and a half hours and cost a third lap its whole allocation to recover. %507's guard is the only reason it was recoverable at all: without a scan that reads records rather than refs, this would have read as an ordinary clean tree.
loom witness=tame_style_long_fn legs=20 fail=0 functions_over_70=321 longest=809 roster_files=611 wall_s=21
loom witness=two_rooms_doorway legs=14 pages=973 research=203 designing=756 docs=14 folded=640 fails=46 ceiling=46 wall_s=25
loom cold_pass=full guards_run=152 green=147 red=2 gated=3 seconds=1192 tree_moved=no
obs A find for the peer whose lane it is, named rather than taken. Their widened stash_record_scan, landed while I was sending, reads paths=24 orphans=2 unread=22 -- and BOTH orphans are shelves that were RENAMED before landing: my own rows-508-509.md, which shipped as rows-511-512.md after three renumbers, and a peer's rows-469.md. The work is landed in each case; the guard reads a basename that no longer exists and calls it orphaned. That is a false positive in the direction that matters least, yet it means the orphan count cannot distinguish a renumbered shelf from a lost file -- and the derived spine guarantees renamed shelves, since an unshared row renumbers by design. Reported never gated, so nothing refuses; theirs to weigh.
loom metric=fascia_v0 before=68 after=58 over70_penalty=25 penalty_cap=25 over70_elder=15 over70_true=321 clutter=42
loom guard=stash_record_widened stashes=3 records=3 landed=3 unlanded=0 paths=24 orphans=2 unread=22 orphans_are_renames=2
loom recovery=stash_record stash_files=22 stash_insertions=919 landed_files=19 landed_insertions=916 renumbers=3 rebases=3
